Q: Height between ListItem rows is too high A: <p>This is what <code>&lt;ListItem /&gt;</code> is interpreted from the browser. The green border marks the padding which got applied - By default it's <code>8</code>, because it's relying on the MUI Base Spacing unit <a href="https://mui.com/material-ui/customization/spacing/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://mui.com/material-ui/customization/spacing/</a>  - there is a shortcut for you</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/J0ln6A2C.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/J0ln6A2C.png" alt="enter image description here" title="ListItem as properties" /></a></p>
<p>Since we pointed out it's the y-axis <code>padding</code>, we can adjust it with mui's <a href="https://mui.com/system/getting-started/the-sx-prop/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sx property</a>, which allows short wiring:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;List&gt;
  {myData.map((obj) =&gt; (
    // py: 0.5 = 0.5 * mui-spacing-value = 4px = dense
    // py: 0.25 = 0.25 * mui-spacing-value = 2px
    &lt;ListItem key={obj.id} sx={{ py: 0.25 }}&gt;
      {obj.label}
    &lt;/ListItem&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/List&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Yup.when: &quot;`NaN` (cast from the value `NaN`)&quot; A: <pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>.typeError(&quot;Amount must be a number&quot;)
.transform((value, originalValue) =&gt; {
  return isNaN(value) ? ([&quot;&quot;, undefined, null].includes(originalValue) ? undefined : new Error()) : value;
})
</code></pre>
<p>Use this, it perfectly solves the problem from both TypeError and empty value. And you can customise the empty values by yourself</p>
